+# Mobile WalletConnect deep-linking: why "Stake" does nothing, why the patch broke it, and the correct pattern
+
+## 1. Why nothing auto-opened before the patch
+
+`writeContractAsync` does not "open a wallet." Over a WalletConnect v2 (WCv2)
+session, it builds an `eth_sendTransaction` JSON-RPC request and **publishes it
+to the WalletConnect relay** — an encrypted message pushed over a websocket to
+the relay server. The wallet holds a persistent relay subscription, so Rainbow
+*receives* the request almost immediately and queues it internally. That is why,
+when the user manually switches to Rainbow, the signing prompt is already sitting
+there. The request landed fine. Nothing was broken on the transport.
+
+The missing piece is **foregrounding**. On mobile the dApp runs in a browser tab
+and the wallet is a separate, backgrounded native app. iOS/Android do **not** let
+a backgrounded app pull itself to the foreground when a websocket message arrives.
+The only thing that can bring Rainbow forward is the app currently in the
+foreground — the browser — navigating to the wallet's **deep link**
+(`rainbow://` / a universal link). That navigation counts as a user-initiated
+app switch, which the OS honors.
+
+RainbowKit/WCv2 are *supposed* to fire that redirect automatically using the
+wallet's redirect metadata stored on the session (`peer.metadata.redirect`).
+On the initial pairing it does (that's the QR/deep-link handoff). On **subsequent
+session requests** this auto-redirect is unreliable and frequently doesn't fire —
+older `@walletconnect` versions, missing `redirect` metadata, or the request
+originating outside a fresh user gesture. Result: the request is on the relay,
+Rainbow has it, but no one told the OS to surface Rainbow. Tap → "nothing happens."
+
+## 2. Why the patch made it worse
+
+```js
+window.location.href = "rainbow://";   // runs first, synchronously
+await writeContractAsync(...);          // runs second — or not at all
+```
+
+Two problems, both caused by inverting the order:
+
+- **You open the wallet before the request exists.** At the instant
+  `location.href` fires, `writeContractAsync` has not run, so nothing has been
+  published to the relay. Rainbow foregrounds and finds an **empty queue** —
+  literally nothing to sign.
+- **Foregrounding Rainbow backgrounds the browser tab, which pauses its JS.**
+  Mobile browsers throttle/suspend background tabs: the event loop and the relay
+  websocket stall. So the `writeContractAsync` publish that was supposed to run
+  "next" is delayed or never completes while the tab is backgrounded. Even once
+  the request eventually goes out, Rainbow is already open and showing nothing.
+
+So the patch reliably wins the race it should lose: wallet first, request never.
+Before the patch the request at least arrived (just without foregrounding);
+after the patch you broke the transport too.
+
+## 3. The correct pattern
+
+### Ordering — fire the request, *don't* await it, then deep-link
+
+`writeContractAsync` only resolves **after** the user approves in the wallet — it
+returns the tx hash post-signature. So you can never `await` it before opening the
+wallet; that's a deadlock (the wallet must open for the user to sign, but you'd be
+waiting for the signature before opening). Instead:
+
+```js
+async function onStake() {
+  if (!shouldDeepLink()) {
+    return writeContractAsync(stakeArgs);   // desktop/injected: just send
+  }
+
+  const scheme = await getWalletRedirect(); // from the session, see below
+
+  // start the request — this publishes to the relay — but do NOT await yet
+  const txPromise = writeContractAsync(stakeArgs);
+
+  // give the relay publish a moment, then foreground the wallet
+  setTimeout(() => {
+    if (scheme) window.location.href = scheme;
+  }, 200);
+
+  return txPromise; // await elsewhere for the hash / receipt
+}
+```
+
+Key point: the JSON-RPC request is published to the relay early in the
+`writeContractAsync` lifecycle, *before* the promise resolves. The short delay
+lets that publish happen, then you foreground a wallet that already has the
+request queued.
+
+### The delay and why its length is bounded on both sides
+
+~150–300 ms. It is a compromise between two hard constraints:
+
+- **Lower bound:** the request must reach the relay before Rainbow foregrounds,
+  or you recreate the patch's "empty queue" bug. A few hundred ms covers the
+  serialize + websocket publish.
+- **Upper bound:** mobile browsers only allow a programmatic app-open
+  (`location.href` to a custom scheme) while the **transient user activation**
+  from the tap is still live. Wait too long and the browser treats the navigation
+  as un-gestured and silently blocks it — the deep link does nothing. Keep it well
+  under a second.
+
+Too short → wallet opens to nothing. Too long → deep link ignored. ~200 ms sits
+in the middle.
+
+### Which scheme to open — never hardcode `rainbow://`
+
+`connector.id === "walletConnect"` for **every** wallet connected via WC —
+MetaMask, Trust, Coinbase, Rainbow all report the same id. It tells you the
+*transport*, not the *wallet*. Hardcoding `rainbow://` sends MetaMask users to
+Rainbow (or nowhere).
+
+Read the wallet's own redirect metadata off the live session:
+
+```js
+async function getWalletRedirect() {
+  const provider = await connector.getProvider();
+  const redirect = provider?.session?.peer?.metadata?.redirect;
+  // prefer the universal link (more reliable on iOS), fall back to native scheme
+  return redirect?.universal || redirect?.native || null;
+}
+```
+
+`native` is the custom scheme (`rainbow://`); `universal` is an https universal
+link (`https://rnbwapp.com/...`). Universal links are generally more reliable on
+iOS, so prefer them when present.
+
+### When to skip the deep link entirely
+
+- **Not a WalletConnect connection** — injected/extension (`connector.id`
+  `injected`/`metaMask`), Coinbase in-app, etc. There's no separate app to
+  foreground; the provider is in-page.
+- **In-app / embedded wallet browser** — the dApp is loaded *inside* MetaMask's,
+  Coinbase's, or Rainbow's own browser. The wallet is already the host and injects
+  its provider; deep-linking would try to leave the in-app browser. Detect via an
+  injected provider being present.
+- **Desktop, including desktop WalletConnect (QR)** — on a QR pairing the wallet
+  is on a *different device*; opening a scheme on the desktop is meaningless.
+  Gate on mobile user-agent / platform.
+- **No redirect metadata on the session** (`peer.metadata.redirect` missing/empty)
+  — you don't know the scheme, so don't guess. Skip rather than fire a blank/wrong
+  scheme that just blanks the page.
+
+```js
+function shouldDeepLink() {
+  return isMobile()                       // not desktop / not QR pairing
+      && connector?.id === "walletConnect"// WC transport, not injected/in-app
+      && !window.ethereum?.isMetaMask;    // not inside an in-app wallet browser
+}
+```
+
+## TL;DR
+
+The request was always reaching Rainbow over the relay; what was missing was
+foregrounding, which only a deep link from the foreground browser can do. The
+patch deep-linked *before* creating the request (and backgrounded the tab that
+still had to send it), so Rainbow opened to an empty queue. The fix: fire
+`writeContractAsync` without awaiting, wait ~200 ms so the request reaches the
+relay while staying inside the tap's activation window, then open the scheme —
+read from the session's `peer.metadata.redirect`, never hardcoded — and skip the
+deep link on desktop, injected/in-app, and metadata-less sessions.

